.TH GETENV 3
.SH NAME
getenv \- value for environment name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B char *getenv(const char *name);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Getenv
searches the environment list
(see
.IR environ (5))
for a string of the form
.IB name = value
and returns
.I value
if such a string is present, otherwise 0 (NULL).
.SH SEE ALSO
environ(5), exec(2), end(3)
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/getenv.c, lib/libc/csu/crt0.s
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS Where environ comes from
Nothing is handed to a program in a register.  Both gates \- the kernel's
.IR exece ()
and b6sim's loader \- lay the argument block at the fixed base
.B 070000
and zero every register but the stack pointer, which is already seeded and must
not be touched.
.PP
.I Crt0
reads
.I argc
from that word and computes
.PP
.RS
.nf
environ = &block[argc + 2]
.fi
.RE
.PP
\- past
.I argc
itself, the
.I argc
pointers, and the null that terminates the
.B argv[]
vector.
.I Environ
is one word of bss defined there.
.SS Every slot strides by one word
A
.B char *
is a fat pointer and carries a byte offset, but a
.B "char **"
is a plain word address \- which is what
.I argv
and
.I envp
are here \- so walking the vector is ordinary word indexing and no decoding is
involved.
.SS The environment is not a good thing to write a test against
lib/test/environ checks
.I getenv
against the vector it was handed, entry by entry, and prints
.B neither a name nor a count.
Under b6sim the environment is a whitelisted slice of the host's, so even
MAKEFLAGS alone would make a count differ between a run under
.I make
and the same run by hand.  An expectation file may record only what the program
does.
